<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qarshi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qarshi is a city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, in the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>. In the 1320s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kebek-khan">Kebek Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Empire</a> attempted to make it a stable sedentary capital. Together with his currency reform, this represented the most serious attempt to transform the khanate into a territorial state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qarshi.jpg" alt="Qarshi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bobyrr · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monument_in_memory_and_honor_in_Qarshi.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The city of <b>Qarshi</b> in Uzbekistan takes its name from the palace that Kebek Khan built there, near Nasaf, in the early 14th century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 555–567, perhaps in 557, the Turks and Khosrow I allied against the Hephthalites and defeated them after an eight-day battle near <b>Qarshi</b>, the Battle of Gol-Zarriun.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
